{
  "dom_id": "step-q154631",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If $|2y+3|=9,$ then $y=$",
  "worked_solution": "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|2y+3|=2y+3,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+3| & =9 \\\\ 2y+3 & =9 \\\\ 2y & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2y+3|=-(2y+3),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2y+3| & =9 \\\\ -(2y+3) & =9 \\\\ -2y-3 & =9 \\\\ -2y & =12 \\\\ \\frac{-2y}{-2} & =\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-6$ and $y=3.$",
